<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>NSMicrophoneUsageDescription</key>
  <string>Papercusp needs microphone access for voice mode, push-to-talk, and wake-word capture.</string>

  <!-- P-302 / EI-20584279536840151 — macOS LOCAL NETWORK PRIVACY (TCC).

       Papercusp's p2p federation (hyperswarm/hyperdht over UDP) dials PEERS ON
       THE LOCAL SUBNET. Since macOS 15, an app that initiates local-network
       traffic must declare this key; without it the system denies that traffic
       and every send fails with EHOSTUNREACH.

       ⚠ DO NOT confuse this with `NSAllowsLocalNetworking` in the ATS dict
       below. That key is App Transport Security — an HTTP/TLS policy for the
       WEBVIEW's plaintext loopback requests. It has NOTHING to do with the TCC
       local-network permission, despite the near-identical name. Having the ATS
       key and missing THIS one is exactly the state that cost ~7h of packet
       archaeology: the DHT was perfectly configured and retrying (918 requests,
       917 timeouts, 0 responses) while the OS dropped every datagram, and
       udx's fire-and-forget `trySend` reported no error to any layer above it.

       The denial is precisely scoped, which is what makes it so deceptive:
       loopback, the host's own address, and the entire PUBLIC internet all
       still work — only local-subnet peers are unreachable. Inbound-initiated
       flows are exempt too, so the node still ANSWERS pings while being unable
       to dial anyone. Guarded by
       macos_local_network_tests::info_plist_declares_local_network_usage. -->
  <key>NSLocalNetworkUsageDescription</key>
  <string>Papercusp connects directly to your other devices on this network to sync your workspaces peer-to-peer.</string>

  <!-- App Transport Security (WI-1802 — packaged-macOS blank page).

       The desktop webview reaches the local operator over PLAINTEXT http on the
       LOOPBACK IP LITERAL: http://127.0.0.1:<dynamic port>. serve binds 127.0.0.1
       ONLY (main.rs: `PAPERCUSP_BIND_HOST=127.0.0.1`, bind `127.0.0.1:{port}`),
       and finish_boot deliberately dials the 127.0.0.1 LITERAL rather than
       `localhost` because WebKitGTK/Wry may resolve `localhost` to ::1 where the
       operator isn't listening (see the comment at main.rs finish_boot).

       macOS App Transport Security blocks plaintext http by default. The prior
       `bundle.macOS.exceptionDomain: "localhost"` in tauri.conf.json generated an
       ATS exception for the HOSTNAME `localhost` ONLY — it does NOT cover the
       127.0.0.1 IP literal the app actually dials. So on packaged macOS builds
       ATS silently blocked every operator request (top-level nav when the custom
       protocol is off, AND the SPA's /api fetch + EventSource always) and the GUI
       rendered a blank white page. Linux (webkit2gtk, no ATS) was unaffected —
       which is exactly why the bug only ever bit the Mac app.

       `NSAllowsLocalNetworking` grants ATS an exception for LOOPBACK (127.0.0.1,
       ::1), link-local, and .local addresses WITHOUT broadly permitting arbitrary
       public http — the surgical, Apple-sanctioned key for a localhost-only
       backend. This dict REPLACES the removed `exceptionDomain` config and is now
       the single source of the app's ATS policy (the `localhost` hostname
       exception is kept explicit below so nothing regresses). Guarded by
       macos_ats_tests::info_plist_allows_loopback_http_for_macos_ats. -->
  <key>NSAppTransportSecurity</key>
  <dict>
    <key>NSAllowsLocalNetworking</key>
    <true/>
    <key>NSExceptionDomains</key>
    <dict>
      <key>localhost</key>
      <dict>
        <key>NSExceptionAllowsInsecureHTTPLoads</key>
        <true/>
        <key>NSIncludesSubdomains</key>
        <true/>
      </dict>
    </dict>
  </dict>
</dict>
</plist>
